	"description": [
		"This course examines how 19th-century Russian male authors and critics used the \"woman question\" to debate broader ethical, societal, and political issues. The course analyzes how these men defined ethical living in an autocratic empire, often erasing female voices from the discourse. Students read major works by Pushkin, Leskov, and Tolstoy, evaluating ethical perspectives while reflecting on the historical marginalization of women in Russian literary culture. (Taught in Russian) Offered periodically. Prerequisite: RUSSN 302 or permission from the instructor."
	],
